WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.325
Lesson zero point eight.

00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:05.620
Your security baseline, and the language of DeFi.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.723
By the end, you'll have ten rules that

00:00:07.723 --> 00:00:10.311
prevent most losses, and every word you'll need

00:00:10.311 --> 00:00:11.875
for the rest of this program.

00:00:13.175 --> 00:00:16.620
Every lesson so far taught you one piece: exchanges,

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.105
your own wallet, networks, your first DeFi steps.

00:00:20.105 --> 00:00:22.626
This lesson is the piece that ties all of

00:00:22.626 --> 00:00:26.048
them together, into ten rules you can actually remember,

00:00:26.048 --> 00:00:29.349
and a shared vocabulary for everything that comes next.

00:00:30.649 --> 00:00:31.939
Here's the plan.

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:34.349
First, all ten rules, grouped into three

00:00:34.349 --> 00:00:37.294
themes you'll actually recall under pressure.

00:00:37.294 --> 00:00:39.832
Second, the full language of DeFi, eighteen

00:00:39.832 --> 00:00:42.554
words you'll meet constantly from here on.

00:00:42.554 --> 00:00:45.045
Third, how a real scam actually unfolds,

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:48.139
and where beginner losses typically come from.

00:00:48.139 --> 00:00:50.302
And finally, a real worked example,

00:00:50.302 --> 00:00:51.864
your checklist and a quiz.

00:00:53.164 --> 00:00:54.199
Don't get scammed.

00:00:55.499 --> 00:00:58.109
Rule one: never share your seed phrase.

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:00.869
No real person or company will ever ask

00:01:00.869 --> 00:01:04.344
for it, not support, not this program, nobody.

00:01:04.344 --> 00:01:07.734
Rule two: bookmark the sites you actually use, and

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:11.191
only ever reach them from that bookmark, never from

00:01:11.191 --> 00:01:13.419
an ad, a DM or an email link.

00:01:13.419 --> 00:01:17.283
Rule three: nobody legitimate DMs you first, offering

00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:21.089
help, an investment, or 'recovery' of lost funds.

00:01:21.089 --> 00:01:23.510
And rule four: guaranteed returns are

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:25.739
a scam, always, with no exceptions.

00:01:27.039 --> 00:01:29.813
Rule four deserves a moment on its own, because

00:01:29.813 --> 00:01:32.886
you'll hear it again and again in later modules.

00:01:32.886 --> 00:01:35.525
Real yield in DeFi moves with markets:

00:01:35.525 --> 00:01:37.554
it goes up, it goes down, and

00:01:37.554 --> 00:01:39.681
it's never locked in stone.

00:01:39.681 --> 00:01:43.413
Anyone promising a fixed, guaranteed number, especially a

00:01:43.413 --> 00:01:46.416
high one, isn't describing a real product.

00:01:46.416 --> 00:01:47.791
They're describing a scam.

00:01:49.091 --> 00:01:50.401
Transaction habits.

00:01:51.701 --> 00:01:55.366
Rule five: read every wallet prompt before approving it,

00:01:55.366 --> 00:01:58.259
what it does, how much, and to whom, exactly

00:01:58.259 --> 00:02:01.066
the habit last lesson was built around.

00:02:01.066 --> 00:02:03.679
Rule six: test first, with a small amount,

00:02:03.679 --> 00:02:05.744
on anything new, a new app, a new

00:02:05.744 --> 00:02:08.171
network, a new kind of transaction.

00:02:08.171 --> 00:02:10.608
And rule seven: use a hardware wallet once

00:02:10.608 --> 00:02:13.441
the amount you're holding actually matters to you.

00:02:14.741 --> 00:02:17.026
Notice these three aren't new ideas.

00:02:17.026 --> 00:02:20.701
Rule five is Lesson zero point seven's whole point.

00:02:20.701 --> 00:02:22.730
Rule six is Lesson zero point

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.141
six's small test transfer, applied everywhere.

00:02:26.141 --> 00:02:28.073
And rule seven is coming properly

00:02:28.073 --> 00:02:29.851
in Lesson one point three.

00:02:29.851 --> 00:02:32.700
This lesson isn't introducing new habits, it's just

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.166
naming the ones you've already been building.

00:02:36.466 --> 00:02:37.686
Everyday hygiene.

00:02:38.986 --> 00:02:41.745
Rule eight: keep your devices updated, and

00:02:41.745 --> 00:02:44.311
use a separate browser profile just for

00:02:44.311 --> 00:02:47.241
crypto, away from your everyday browsing.

00:02:47.241 --> 00:02:49.583
Rule nine: keep records of every buy,

00:02:49.583 --> 00:02:52.419
sell, transfer and fee, you'll thank yourself

00:02:52.419 --> 00:02:54.506
later, including at tax time.

00:02:54.506 --> 00:02:57.038
And rule ten, the one that catches almost

00:02:57.038 --> 00:03:00.291
everything else: if something feels urgent, stop.

00:03:00.291 --> 00:03:03.076
Scammers manufacture urgency on purpose.

00:03:03.076 --> 00:03:05.971
Real opportunities can always wait for you to check.

00:03:07.271 --> 00:03:09.352
If you remember only one rule under

00:03:09.352 --> 00:03:11.501
real pressure, make it rule ten.

00:03:11.501 --> 00:03:13.486
A wallet can't be 'suspended'.

00:03:13.486 --> 00:03:16.386
A giveaway doesn't expire in the next ten minutes.

00:03:16.386 --> 00:03:18.637
Urgency is not a feature of a real

00:03:18.637 --> 00:03:22.883
opportunity, it's a manufactured pressure tactic, and recognising

00:03:22.883 --> 00:03:24.941
it is usually enough on its own.

00:03:26.241 --> 00:03:28.061
Rule nine deserves a moment too,

00:03:28.061 --> 00:03:30.511
because it doesn't pay off immediately.

00:03:30.511 --> 00:03:32.718
Good records mean you can reconcile

00:03:32.718 --> 00:03:35.163
a discrepancy in minutes, not days.

00:03:35.163 --> 00:03:37.198
They're the first thing a legitimate

00:03:37.198 --> 00:03:39.368
support process actually asks for.

00:03:39.368 --> 00:03:43.000
And in most places, they're exactly what's needed at

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.658
tax time anyway, covered properly in Lesson 12.7.

00:03:46.658 --> 00:03:48.969
Build the habit before you have hundreds

00:03:48.969 --> 00:03:51.393
of transactions to reconstruct from memory.

00:03:52.693 --> 00:03:54.128
The language of DeFi.

00:03:55.428 --> 00:03:57.523
Four words about who holds what.

00:03:57.523 --> 00:03:58.982
Your address is your public

00:03:58.982 --> 00:04:00.898
account number on a blockchain.

00:04:00.898 --> 00:04:04.071
Your seed phrase is twelve to twenty-four words that

00:04:04.071 --> 00:04:07.963
recreate your wallet's keys, never shared with anyone, ever.

00:04:07.963 --> 00:04:11.099
Your private key is the actual secret that signs

00:04:11.099 --> 00:04:14.728
transactions; your seed phrase is what generates it.

00:04:14.728 --> 00:04:17.608
And custody just means who holds those keys:

00:04:17.608 --> 00:04:21.383
an exchange, that's custodial, or you, that's self-custody.

00:04:22.683 --> 00:04:24.938
Five words about the network you're on.

00:04:24.938 --> 00:04:27.334
Gas is the fee a network charges for

00:04:27.334 --> 00:04:30.743
a transaction, paid in that network's own token.

00:04:30.743 --> 00:04:34.096
A network, or chain, is simply which blockchain you're

00:04:34.096 --> 00:04:37.628
using right now: Ethereum, Arbitrum, Base, and so on.

00:04:37.628 --> 00:04:39.595
A Layer 2 is a cheaper, faster

00:04:39.595 --> 00:04:41.988
network built on top of Ethereum.

00:04:41.988 --> 00:04:44.104
A token is a unit of value

00:04:44.104 --> 00:04:47.618
living on a blockchain, USDC for example.

00:04:47.618 --> 00:04:50.596
And a stablecoin is a token deliberately designed

00:04:50.596 --> 00:04:53.158
to hold a steady value, usually one dollar.

00:04:54.458 --> 00:04:56.603
Four words about DeFi itself.

00:04:56.603 --> 00:04:59.336
A smart contract is a program on a blockchain

00:04:59.336 --> 00:05:03.023
that follows fixed rules, there's no negotiating with it.

00:05:03.023 --> 00:05:06.086
A DEX, a decentralised exchange, lets you

00:05:06.086 --> 00:05:09.448
swap tokens directly from your own wallet.

00:05:09.448 --> 00:05:12.155
An approval is permission for an app to move

00:05:12.155 --> 00:05:15.523
a specific token, the whole subject of last lesson.

00:05:15.523 --> 00:05:17.756
And a block explorer is a website that

00:05:17.756 --> 00:05:20.963
shows every transaction that's ever happened, in public.

00:05:22.263 --> 00:05:24.948
And five more, about numbers and access.

00:05:24.948 --> 00:05:27.208
A testnet is a practice blockchain; a

00:05:27.208 --> 00:05:29.230
faucet is the site that hands you

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:30.898
free test coins for it.

00:05:30.898 --> 00:05:33.203
APY is annual percentage yield,

00:05:33.203 --> 00:05:36.528
your yearly return, including compounding.

00:05:36.528 --> 00:05:39.004
Liquidity is how easily something can be

00:05:39.004 --> 00:05:41.720
bought or sold without moving its price.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:46.430
KYC is the identity checks regulated exchanges require.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:50.197
And two-factor authentication, 2FA, is a second proof

00:05:50.197 --> 00:05:53.545
of identity at login, beyond just your password.

00:05:54.844 --> 00:05:56.274
That's all eighteen.

00:05:56.274 --> 00:05:58.013
You won't memorise them from one

00:05:58.013 --> 00:05:59.789
pass, and you don't need to.

00:05:59.789 --> 00:06:02.857
You'll meet every single one of these words again,

00:06:02.857 --> 00:06:05.684
in context, starting with the very next module.

00:06:06.984 --> 00:06:08.509
Spotting it before it happens.

00:06:09.809 --> 00:06:12.407
Here's the anatomy of a typical D.M.

00:06:12.407 --> 00:06:15.164
scam, almost always the same shape.

00:06:15.164 --> 00:06:17.562
It opens with a message claiming to be

00:06:17.562 --> 00:06:20.874
support, or a giveaway, or someone offering help.

00:06:20.874 --> 00:06:22.826
It adds urgency: act in the next

00:06:22.826 --> 00:06:25.669
ten minutes, or your wallet is 'suspended'.

00:06:25.669 --> 00:06:28.326
It dangles a fake guaranteed return, or

00:06:28.326 --> 00:06:31.049
free money, to override your caution, no

00:06:31.049 --> 00:06:33.209
real product works that way.

00:06:33.209 --> 00:06:35.781
And it ends with a request, dressed up as

00:06:35.781 --> 00:06:39.694
'verification': your seed phrase, or an unlimited approval.

00:06:39.694 --> 00:06:41.788
Every single step in that chain breaks

00:06:41.788 --> 00:06:43.614
one of the rules you just learned.

00:06:44.914 --> 00:06:48.511
And here's roughly where beginner losses actually come from,

00:06:48.511 --> 00:06:51.754
purely as an illustrative breakdown, to show why every

00:06:51.754 --> 00:06:54.354
lesson in this module earned its place.

00:06:54.354 --> 00:06:55.839
Seed-phrase phishing.

00:06:55.839 --> 00:06:58.744
Fake urgency and impersonated support.

00:06:58.744 --> 00:07:00.843
Unlimited approvals left open on

00:07:00.843 --> 00:07:02.734
an app you stopped using.

00:07:02.734 --> 00:07:05.654
And skipping the small test transfer on something new.

00:07:05.654 --> 00:07:07.600
Notice: rules one, ten, five

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.144
and six, in that order.

00:07:10.444 --> 00:07:12.762
Notice that every slice of that chart

00:07:12.762 --> 00:07:15.445
already has its own dedicated defence, from

00:07:15.445 --> 00:07:17.819
a lesson you've already completed.

00:07:17.819 --> 00:07:20.497
Rule ten, stop when something feels urgent,

00:07:20.497 --> 00:07:22.626
would have caught almost all of it

00:07:22.626 --> 00:07:25.061
anyway, on its own, without you needing

00:07:25.061 --> 00:07:27.434
to identify which specific scam it was.

00:07:28.734 --> 00:07:31.406
One more thing worth knowing: every category

00:07:31.406 --> 00:07:33.365
on that chart gets a full lesson

00:07:33.365 --> 00:07:35.624
of its own, later in the program.

00:07:35.624 --> 00:07:38.498
Lesson 1.6 is entirely dedicated to

00:07:38.498 --> 00:07:41.034
scam defence, in real depth.

00:07:41.034 --> 00:07:42.982
For now, the ten rules you already

00:07:42.982 --> 00:07:45.098
have are enough to recognise all four

00:07:45.098 --> 00:07:46.879
of them the moment they show up.

00:07:48.179 --> 00:07:49.584
A real worked example.

00:07:50.884 --> 00:07:53.432
Jordan gets a DM: 'Your wallet has been

00:07:53.432 --> 00:07:56.108
flagged and will be suspended in one hour

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:59.039
unless you verify it now.' It looks official,

00:07:59.039 --> 00:08:00.804
and it includes a link.

00:08:00.804 --> 00:08:02.409
Jordan doesn't click it.

00:08:02.409 --> 00:08:07.319
Rule three fires first: nobody legitimate DMs you first.

00:08:07.319 --> 00:08:10.460
Rule ten fires right behind it: the one-hour

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:14.599
countdown is manufactured urgency, not a real deadline.

00:08:14.599 --> 00:08:17.720
Jordan opens their wallet directly, from a bookmark,

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:20.370
not the link, and confirms nothing is wrong,

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.496
wallets can't be 'suspended' in the first place.

00:08:23.496 --> 00:08:25.481
The message gets blocked and reported.

00:08:26.782 --> 00:08:28.728
Notice what made that safe wasn't

00:08:28.728 --> 00:08:31.832
cleverness, or spotting some sophisticated trick.

00:08:31.832 --> 00:08:34.310
It was two rules, recognised instantly,

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:37.087
because Jordan already knew their names.

00:08:37.087 --> 00:08:39.180
That's the entire point of turning

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:41.332
ten habits into ten memorable rules.

00:08:42.632 --> 00:08:43.812
Checklist and quiz.

00:08:45.112 --> 00:08:47.007
Here's this lesson's checklist.

00:08:47.007 --> 00:08:48.545
You've read the ten rules, and

00:08:48.545 --> 00:08:50.432
can repeat them without looking.

00:08:50.432 --> 00:08:53.542
You've set up a separate browser profile for crypto.

00:08:53.542 --> 00:08:55.464
And you recognise every word in the

00:08:55.464 --> 00:08:57.472
glossary when you meet it again.

00:08:57.472 --> 00:09:00.922
Tick all three, and Module zero is genuinely complete.

00:09:02.222 --> 00:09:03.432
Question one.

00:09:03.432 --> 00:09:06.217
Someone offers a 'guaranteed two percent a day'.

00:09:06.217 --> 00:09:06.942
What is it?

00:09:11.362 --> 00:09:13.077
The answer: a scam.

00:09:13.077 --> 00:09:15.578
Guaranteed returns simply don't exist;

00:09:15.578 --> 00:09:17.502
real yield moves with markets.

00:09:18.802 --> 00:09:20.047
Question two.

00:09:20.047 --> 00:09:20.922
What's gas?

00:09:25.342 --> 00:09:27.391
The answer: the network fee for a

00:09:27.391 --> 00:09:30.162
transaction, paid in that network's own token.

00:09:31.462 --> 00:09:32.732
Question three.

00:09:32.732 --> 00:09:34.733
A message says your wallet will be

00:09:34.733 --> 00:09:37.262
'suspended' unless you act in one hour.

00:09:37.262 --> 00:09:38.112
What do you do?

00:09:42.532 --> 00:09:44.127
The answer: stop.

00:09:44.127 --> 00:09:46.157
Urgency is a scam tactic.

00:09:46.157 --> 00:09:48.614
Wallets can't actually be suspended; check

00:09:48.614 --> 00:09:50.957
only through a bookmarked, official site.

00:09:52.257 --> 00:09:53.512
Question four.

00:09:53.512 --> 00:09:56.467
What's the difference between custodial and self-custody?

00:10:00.887 --> 00:10:03.654
The answer: custodial means an exchange holds

00:10:03.654 --> 00:10:06.181
your keys for you; self-custody means you

00:10:06.181 --> 00:10:08.527
hold them yourself, in your own wallet.

00:10:09.827 --> 00:10:11.142
Question five.

00:10:11.142 --> 00:10:13.534
Which single rule would have caught almost every

00:10:13.534 --> 00:10:15.487
scam in this lesson's chart, on its own?

00:10:19.907 --> 00:10:21.722
The answer: rule ten.

00:10:21.722 --> 00:10:23.647
If something feels urgent, stop.

00:10:24.947 --> 00:10:27.757
Here's the whole lesson, recapped as one loop.

00:10:27.757 --> 00:10:30.378
Protect: never share your seed phrase, and

00:10:30.378 --> 00:10:33.117
treat every guaranteed return as a scam.

00:10:33.117 --> 00:10:35.773
Verify: read every prompt, test small, and

00:10:35.773 --> 00:10:38.482
stop the moment something feels urgent.

00:10:38.482 --> 00:10:42.687
Maintain: keep devices updated and keep records, always.

00:10:42.687 --> 00:10:44.982
Do those three things, in that order,

00:10:44.982 --> 00:10:47.639
and you've built the security baseline this

00:10:47.639 --> 00:10:49.692
entire program is designed around.

00:10:50.992 --> 00:10:54.677
This is education, not financial advice, and every dollar

00:10:54.677 --> 00:10:58.407
figure and every chart in this lesson is illustrative.

00:10:58.407 --> 00:11:01.577
That said, this specific lesson matters more than

00:11:01.577 --> 00:11:04.366
most: nobody from this program, or any real

00:11:04.366 --> 00:11:06.966
one, will ever ask for your seed phrase,

00:11:06.966 --> 00:11:09.231
private keys or account access.

00:11:09.231 --> 00:11:10.036
Not once.

00:11:11.336 --> 00:11:14.572
That's your security baseline, and the language of

00:11:14.572 --> 00:11:17.601
DeFi, and with it, Module zero is complete.

00:11:17.601 --> 00:11:20.554
Next, Module one begins properly: what DeFi

00:11:20.554 --> 00:11:23.573
actually is, and the risk-first mindset that

00:11:23.573 --> 00:11:26.056
runs through everything from here on.
